// The plan for the monitor's own mode on a Polaris card (DCE 11.2): from
// what the display controller was read to run (gpu_display.mlx) and the
// monitor's own timing (edid.mlx), the list of what would be written, in
// order, each word worked out beforehand. Nothing here writes: the card is
// reached through gpu_display's `Card`, which can only read. The plan is
// made and said at every boot; carrying it out is gpu_set.mlx's, and only
// when the image asks (settings/gpu).
//
// Two situations (gpu_display.mlx's head):
// (a) the controller runs the monitor's own timing and the card scales
//     the firmware's small picture: a surface of the timing's size, the
//     viewport, the scaler off, the line buffer, on the running
//     controller;
// (b) the controller runs the small picture's timing: the pixel clock,
//     the timing and what (a) sets.
//     By DisplayPort only when the link the firmware trained has room for
//     the new clock: on this controller a DisplayPort stream's pixel clock
//     is made by a DTO (a phase over a modulo of a reference clock), which
//     is a register; the link's own clock and its training are the PHY's,
//     set by the card's firmware tables (not in a plan yet).
//     By TMDS (HDMI or DVI signalling) the pixel clock is a PLL's and the
//     link's clock is the pixel clock: the output is taken down, the PLL
//     set, the timing set and the output brought up again, and the PLL,
//     the transmitter, the timing and the controller's start and stop are
//     the card's own firmware tables' work, as on Linux (atom.mlx runs
//     them; gpu_blocks.mlx says what each is told). "ON A TMDS OUTPUT"
//     below says the sequence, what it is taken from and where it leaves
//     Linux.
//
// The order and the locks are Linux 6.6's display core for this
// controller (drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc), function by function as
// the steps below name them, with the older amdgpu/dce_v11_0.c where the
// display core leaves a word alone that the firmware set for narrow rows.
// Where Linux hands a step to the card's firmware tables, the registers
// are written directly, by what its own code says they hold, and the step
// says so. Every register number and field is from Linux's headers
// (asic_reg/dce/dce_11_2_d.h and dce_11_2_sh_mask.h), each looked up and
// held against them by tools/check_gpu_registers.py.
//
// Left as the firmware has them: the surface's pixel format and tiling
// (GRPH_CONTROL, GRPH_SWAP_CNTL: the new picture is drawn as the old one
// is), the colour path, the line buffer's pixel depth, the stream's pixel
// format, the stutter and memory clock marks, and the link.
//
// ON A TMDS OUTPUT. The sequence is the display core's for a stream whose
// timing changes on a running output (dc_commit_state_no_check ->
// dce110_apply_ctx_to_hw: dce110_reset_hw_ctx_wrap takes the old stream
// down, apply_single_controller_ctx_to_hw brings the new one up,
// dce110_apply_ctx_for_surface sets the picture), each step naming its
// function where it is made below (`tmdsThere`; the way back:
// `tmdsBack`). In short:
//   the urgency marks at their highest; the firmware's two scratch words
//   as Linux has them for every table; the display engine's clock raised
//   if it is too low (a table, and the DisplayPort reference clock's
//   after it);
//   down: an HDMI output muted and its packets stopped, the front end
//   taken off the back end, the transmitter off (a table), the
//   controller blanked and stopped (a table), its buffers given back;
//   the clock and the timing: the PLL (a table), the timing (a table),
//   the controller started (a table), and its frames counted with the
//   transmitter still off: is the clock the one asked for?
//   up: the back end's mode, the stream (a table), for HDMI the packets
//   and the AVI info frame, the transmitter on (a table), the mode once
//   more, an HDMI output unmuted;
//   the picture as in (a), shown last.
// Linux has no way back from a mode set that fails; here the way back is
// the same sequence run towards the firmware's state, worked out with the
// plan (`back`): the tables are told the firmware's timing, mode and
// clock (the clock as it was measured, a PLL cannot be read), and every
// register the plan writes itself gets the firmware's value again.
// What is ours and not Linux's, each for care and said where it is made:
// the checks after the tables (the transmitter's and the controller's
// state waited for, the clock's source and the timing's words read, the
// frames counted before the monitor is sent anything), a pause so that
// the monitor sees the signal gone, the display engine's clock kept when
// it is high enough, DVI signalling kept for a clock a DVI link carries
// when the firmware's picture is sent that way, the monitor's own
// highest clock taken as a limit, and no change at all above what a link
// carries without scrambling (the monitor would have to be told over its
// DDC lines, and this driver has no I2C). Left out of Linux's sequence:
// its power gating table before the controller is set (the controller
// runs the firmware's picture: it is not gated), the transmitter table's
// "init" at the driver's start, audio, stereo sync, the test pattern and
// static screen words, the HDMI status and control channel's word.
//
// THE FIRMWARE'S OWN MODE SET AGAIN (a rehearsal: `Wish.rehearsal`): the
// same steps with the firmware's own timing, mode and clock as the
// target, and the picture left where it is. Nothing new is asked of the
// monitor; it shows that the card's tables run here, that the output goes
// off and comes back and that the checks hold on this card, before the
// monitor's own mode is tried. (It does not show that the PLL takes
// another clock: the clock asked for is the one that runs.)
const scanout = @import("./gpu_display.mlx")
const edid = @import("./edid.mlx")
const atom = @import("./atom.mlx")
const videorom = @import("./gpu_rom.mlx")
const blocks = @import("./gpu_blocks.mlx")

